---
/**
 * BothSides — homepage section 4, "Both sides of the table".
 *
 * DESIGN: B2 "Shared spine, both ends", Figma frame 78:1065 on page
 * `Section — Both sides` (78:3). Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in
 * specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md. Six variants were rendered; this is the one
 * taken, and it is not the one I recommended. Build it, do not re-argue it.
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT THE GEOMETRY MAKES. One raised rail carries three station caps —
 * Requirement, Evaluation, After award. The supplier lane sits above it and
 * reads left to right; the authority lane sits below it and reads right to
 * left. Two readers, opposite directions, the same spine. That is the whole
 * claim: a commitment means the same thing from either chair. Any future edit
 * that flattens this into two side-by-side cards throws the argument away and
 * keeps the words.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY THE OLD WORDING IS NOT CARRIED OVER. The previous
 * build was two prose cards gated on OA-01, because the section once led with
 * "the only engine that sits on both sides" and closed with "almost nothing
 * else in this market does both" — an absolute uniqueness claim, stated twice,
 * with nothing behind it. B2 carries no competitor claim and no figures at all,
 * so there is nothing here to source and nothing to hedge. The National TOMs
 * form of words that the old cards needed is not needed here because this
 * section no longer describes the pricing mechanism; that hedge now lives in
 * full on /sources, under "National TOMs".
 *
 * NO ARROW GLYPHS IN MONO, and this is a real defect not a preference.
 * JetBrains Mono has no arrow coverage, so `→` falls back to a substitute glyph
 * and rendered as a stray `+` in the first build of this section. The lane
 * labels use `>>` and `<<`, which the face does have. Do not put an arrow
 * character inside anything using --font-mono anywhere on this site.
 *
 * THE ROWS ALIGN WITHOUT SUBGRID, deliberately. Each station is its own grid
 * with rows `1fr auto auto auto auto 1fr`. Stations are grid items in one row,
 * so they stretch to a common height; the four `auto` rows hold fixed-height
 * decoration, so the two `1fr` rows resolve to the same size in every station.
 * The rail therefore lands on the same y in all three columns by construction,
 * whatever the copy does. Subgrid would also work and is ~93% supported; this
 * needs no fallback path at all.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT, AND NOTHING WAITING ON AN OBSERVER. Every word is in the HTML
 * and painted at first render. This section is one of the four that once
 * shipped permanently invisible on this site because their content was gated on
 * an IntersectionObserver that never fired.
 *
 * ── THE COLOUR PASS OF 2026-08-05 (A-104 item 7, A-113, A-111) ──────────────
 *
 * Owner: "Both sides of the table aligned to the current colour style."
 *
 * WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, COUNTED RATHER THAN DESCRIBED. Six things in this
 * section were painted in a MARK colour while asserting nothing: the authority
 * lane label, the three authority readings, the rail, the two connector ticks,
 * the station cap's halo and both travelling lights. Teal on this site means
 * one thing — a figure the engine traced back to a recorded commitment — and
 * orchid means one thing, refused. Neither of those was being said anywhere in
 * this frame except on the words "or dropped".
 *
 * SO THE SECTION NOW HAS ONE FOCAL ACCENT AND IT IS THE REFUSAL. That is
 * A-113's rule applied here: a premium dark interface spends peak saturation
 * once per section, on the thing that matters. Everything drawn is white at a
 * value step, which is also what makes the one orchid word carry.
 *
 * IT IS NOT A DESATURATION OF THE PAGE. Two sections down, ReasoningTrace keeps
 * a teal rail and teal dots, because there every dot IS a step the engine
 * completed, and that is the distinction one-semantic-per-hue exists to
 * protect: the same hue in the same treatment either means something or it is
 * decoration, and it cannot be both on one page.
 *
 * WHAT IS LEFT AND IS NOT MINE TO TAKE. The two ambient fields behind this
 * section still resolve from --orb-teal and --orb-violet. Those two tokens are
 * the shared rig for six sections plus body::before and body::after; changing
 * them here alone would make this section the only one lit differently, which
 * is the per-page opt-out the master design directive forbids. It is one
 * decision for tokens.css, recorded rather than half-taken.
 *
 * AND THE AMBIENT MOTION IS DOWN TO THE ENTRANCE. A-111 measured NINE perpetual
 * animations in this section at 1440: six beam travels, one cap bloom, two
 * ambient drifts. The seven that belong to this file now fire once, on the same
 * `data-lit` reveal, with every duration and every delay unchanged. The two
 * that remain are the shared `[data-field] > b` drift in tokens.css, which is
 * the same one decision as the paragraph above.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Station {
  /** The station cap: one moment in the same contract. */
  name: string;
  /** Read from above, left to right. */
  supplier: string;
  /** Read from below, right to left. */
  authority: string;
  /**
   * A trailing clause marked as refused rather than verified. Held as its own
   * field rather than matched out of `authority` at render time: a string
   * replace would fail silently the first time somebody reworded one of the two
   * and not the other, and it would fail by dropping the highlight, which is
   * the one thing on this rail carrying meaning. Orchid is the refusal colour
   * sitewide and is never used decoratively.
   */
  refused?: string;
}

/**
 * THREE MOMENTS, NOT THREE STEPS, so they are not numbered. This is the same
 * contract seen at three points on one timeline; a 01/02/03 rail would assert a
 * sequence the reader has to follow. ReasoningTrace numbers its five steps
 * because those genuinely are ordered — you cannot check a figure you have not
 * computed.
 */
const STATIONS: Station[] = [
  {
    name: 'Requirement',
    supplier: 'Which measures apply',
    authority: 'Publish the rubric',
  },
  {
    name: 'Evaluation',
    supplier: 'Answer from your library',
    authority: 'Located word for word, ',
    refused: 'or dropped',
  },
  {
    name: 'After award',
    supplier: 'Deliver what was priced',
    authority: 'Audit the commitment',
  },
];

/*
 * THE PROOF ROW IS GONE, AND SO IS THE CLOSING LINE (owner review, 2026-08-02,
 * second pass: "Same issues with 'Both sides of the table' ... we are giving so
 * much textual information", and the verdict that the page reads as research
 * rather than product).
 *
 * A mono row reading "PROOF POINT · UK PUBLIC · PROCUREMENT ACT 2023 s.52 · PPN
 * 002 · NATIONAL TOMS CONVENTIONS" is a citation strip, and the standing rule is
 * now that the homepage carries none. Every one of those four instruments is on
 * /sources with its dates, its threshold and a primary-source link, which is
 * more than this row ever gave: it named them and stopped.
 *
 * The closing line, "The same measures, whichever chair you read them from",
 * restated the heading and the standfirst in a third form. Cover it with your
 * hand and the rail read from both ends still makes the point, so it is
 * commentary and it went.
 *
 * s.52 and s.71 remain separate duties everywhere they appear (OA-26). Nothing
 * about that changed here, because nothing here names either of them any more.
 */
---

{/*
  ── A STANDFIRST, RESTORED 2026-08-04, AND IT REVERSES A RECORDED DECISION ───

  This block used to read: "NO STANDFIRST. It said in three clauses what the two
  lane labels, the arrow directions and the rail say by construction. The
  heading names the idea and the drawing performs it, which is the test this
  pass applied to every section." That was written in the 2026-08-02 pass whose
  verdict was that the page read as research rather than product, and it was the
  right call for the standfirst it deleted, which was a prose transcript of the
  drawing directly below it.

  The owner's instruction of 2026-08-04 is that this section keeps its drawing
  and gains "slightly more text". So the sentence that comes back is NOT the one
  that went: it is the concept's, and it argues something the rail cannot. The
  rail shows two readers travelling in opposite directions along one spine. What
  it does not say is WHY that matters, which is that the passage the supplier
  writes and the passage the authority bands are the same passage and ought to
  be cited the same way on both screens. That is a claim about the product, in
  two sentences, and nothing in the drawing states it.

  The three things the 2026-08-02 pass removed are still gone and should stay
  gone: the citation strip, the closing line that restated the heading, and the
  original standfirst. This is one addition, not a restoration of that pass.
*/}
{/* `sv-floor` is the second surface step, on the <section> so it runs the full
    viewport width. Sections alternate floor and base down the page: the ring
    above is base, this is floor, the reasoning trace below is base again. See
    styles/surfaces.css and specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3. */}
<Section
  eyebrow="Both sides of the table"
  title="Read from either end."
  standfirst="The supplier writing the answer and the authority evaluating it are working on the same passage. It should be the same passage, cited the same way, on both screens."
  id="both-sides"
  class="sv-floor"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.35, per `146:3`. Rail drawn, three caps lit, both
    lanes readable before this attribute means anything. If motion.ts never
    runs it stays unset and the section is the finished diagram.
  */}
  <div class="bs" data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.35">
    {/*
      THE SECTION'S ARGUMENT USED TO BE LIT RATHER THAN CAPTIONED (`146:174`),
      AND THAT WAS THE DEFECT. The supplier lane was keyed teal from ABOVE and
      the authority lane violet from BELOW, and the note here argued that
      neither hue marked anything because swapping them left the claim intact.

      THE TEST WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. It asked whether the hues meant
      anything HERE. What matters is what they already mean everywhere else:
      teal is verified and orchid is refused on every other section of this
      page, so keying a lane teal teaches a reader that the supplier side is
      the verified one. A hue that can be swapped is a hue that should not have
      been spent. One field, one hue, per the owner's rule of 2026-08-05; the
      lanes are told apart by their labels, their direction and the spine,
      which is how the section reads with no light at all.
    */}
    <div class="bs__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="bs__amb-a"></b>
      {/* The cursor light and the grain, inside the wrapper so both stay behind
          every word and inherit its mask. <i>, so neither joins the drift. */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    {/*
      The lane label is the diagram's only orientation cue, so the direction is
      written as words for a screen reader and drawn as chevrons for a sighted
      reader. The chevrons are aria-hidden: `>>` announced literally is noise.
    */}
    <p class="meta bs__lane bs__lane--sup">
      Supplier reads<span class="bs__dir" aria-hidden="true">&gt;&gt;</span>
      <span class="visually-hidden">left to right, from above the spine</span>
    </p>

    {/*
      THE RAIL STANDS ON A RAISED PANEL. specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.4: seven
      sections on one background is the reason the page has no depth to read,
      and the answer measured against the closest dark comparator is a value
      ladder rather than a lighter page. `sv-raised` is the third of four steps,
      defined once in styles/surfaces.css and not restated here.
    */}
    {/*
      THE PANEL IS A <figure> AND THE RAIL IS STILL AN <ol>, AND BOTH HALVES OF
      THAT MATTER.

      A figure, because this is a drawing: three stations on a rail, with the
      supplier reading pinned above each one and the authority reading below it.
      Those readings are labels attached to the geometry beside them, so a rule
      that asked them to sit on the panel's centre line would take them off the
      thing they label. scripts/check-render.js excludes <figure> for exactly
      that reason, and marking the drawing as a drawing is the honest way to
      claim the exclusion rather than an entry in its allow-list.

      An <ol>, because the three stations are a sequence and that sequence is
      announced. The panel treatment could not simply move onto the list: the
      list IS the ordered content, and a <figure> element cannot hold list
      semantics. So the panel wraps the list rather than replacing it, which
      also puts the travelling-light markup somewhere valid — a <span> as a
      direct child of an <ol> is not allowed by the HTML parser's content model,
      and that is what the sibling rails were doing.
    */}
    <figure class="bs__panel sv-raised">
    <ol class="bs__spine">
      {
        STATIONS.map((s, i) => (
          <li class="bs__station" data-mid={i === 1 ? '' : undefined}>
            {/*
              A PARAGRAPH, NOT AN <h3>, AND THE TAG NOW MATCHES WHAT IT IS.
              Sitewide type audit, 2026-08-02: this was the last off-scale
              heading recipe on the site, an h3 rendered at --t-micro, weight
              500, mono, uppercase, inside a pill. Two fixes were available:
              size it as a heading, or stop calling it one.

              It is not a heading. A heading introduces the content beneath it,
              and this chip does not: it is the station's NAME on a rail, and
              the two readings either side of it belong to the station rather
              than being introduced by its label. Each reading already carries
              its own visually-hidden "Supplier:" or "Authority:" prefix, and
              the <ol>/<li> structure carries the grouping, so nothing is lost
              from the accessibility tree by dropping the heading level. What is
              gained is that the site now has four heading sizes and no fifth
              recipe hiding behind a pill.
            */}
            <p class="chip bs__chip">{s.name}</p>

            <p class="bs__read bs__read--sup">
              <span class="visually-hidden">Supplier: </span>
              {s.supplier}
            </p>

            {/* Decoration. The rail and its cap carry no information that the
                text above and below does not already carry in full. */}
            <span class="bs__tick bs__tick--up" aria-hidden="true" />
            <span class="bs__railcell" aria-hidden="true">
              {/*
                TWO LIGHTS, ONE PER READER, AND THE RAIL IS ALREADY DRAWN UNDER
                THEM. Each station owns its own third of both travels: A runs
                left to right and takes its third at 0, 800 and 1,600ms; B runs
                right to left and takes the same thirds in the opposite order.
                Three equal cells, one clock, so the two cross dead centre at
                1,200ms without either of them knowing the rail's total width.
                That is why this is per-cell rather than one overlay: the rail's
                y position falls out of a grid nothing can measure in CSS.
              */}
              <span class="bs__track">
                <span class="bs__beam bs__beam--a" />
                <span class="bs__beam bs__beam--b" />
              </span>
              <span class="bs__cap" />
            </span>
            <span class="bs__tick bs__tick--down" aria-hidden="true" />

            <p class="bs__read bs__read--auth">
              <span class="visually-hidden">Authority: </span>
              {s.authority}
              {s.refused && <span class="bs__refused">{s.refused}</span>}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    </figure>

    <p class="meta bs__lane bs__lane--auth">
      <span class="bs__dir" aria-hidden="true">&lt;&lt;</span>Authority reads
      <span class="visually-hidden">right to left, from below the spine</span>
    </p>

    {/*
      DEPTH BEHIND THE DIAGRAM. The Figma shows a single "Learn more", and the
      standing target for this pass is one link per section. This is the one
      section with a DOCUMENTED reason to carry two: it has two audiences, the
      whole argument is that they are different chairs, and a single generic
      link cannot serve both. Dropping the buyer link would also strand
      /crowmark-buyers, which the content-parity allow-list names as the page
      still carrying the buyer proposition in full. Two links, one per lane, and
      each names its own destination rather than saying "Learn more".
    */}
    <p class="bs__more">
      <a href="/crowmark">For suppliers</a>
      <a href="/crowmark-buyers">For buyers</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .bs {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* ── Ambient: two keys, opposite sides ───────────────────────────────────*/
  /* Bleed exactly one gutter, never a percentage. See MarketShape.astro for
     the arithmetic: a percentage bleed overflowed the page at 390 and 768. */
  .bs__amb {
    position: absolute;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw, and no two consecutive fields share one.
       See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 30px;
    inset-block: -18%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. See the full note
       in MarketShape.astro, including the mistake of masking only one axis.  */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did — but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side — exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .bs__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* ONE FIELD, AND THE PAIR THAT WENT WAS THE WORST OF THE SIX.
     It was `bs__amb-sup`, teal from above with the supplier, and
     `bs__amb-auth`, violet from below with the authority: the site's
     verified/refused pair used a third time, as a SIDE MARKER, on the section
     whose entire argument is that the two sides read the same measures. That
     is A-113's objection in its sharpest form — teal cannot mean verified if
     it also means supplier — and the owner's rule of 2026-08-05 settles it by
     allowing one hue per section.

     So there is one field now, taking the high-left geometry the supplier
     light had and the violet the site's atmosphere is standardising on. The
     class is `bs__amb-a` rather than either old name because neither side owns
     it any more; that is the whole point of the change and a name that still
     said `sup` would undo it in the next reader's head. */
  .bs__amb-a {
    width: 620px;
    height: 380px;
    left: 2%;
    top: -22%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .bs__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 260px; }
  }

  /* ── Lane labels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .bs__lane {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .bs__lane--sup { justify-content: flex-start; }

  /*
   * The bottom lane label needs its own clearance and the top one does not.
   * The spine's first row is `1fr` with the supplier text pushed to its bottom
   * edge, so there is always slack above it; the last row is `1fr` with the
   * authority text pushed to its TOP edge, so the label lands directly against
   * the final line. Measured at 1440: 9px between "the commitment" and the
   * label, which reads as a collision rather than a lane marker.
   */
  /*
   * IT USED TO SET `color: var(--c-verified)` AND THAT WAS TEAL SAYING NOTHING.
   * A lane label is an orientation cue: it tells a reader which direction this
   * half of the drawing is read in. It makes no assertion that anything has
   * been verified, and the standing rule is one semantic per hue — teal means a
   * figure the engine traced back to a recorded commitment, and nothing else
   * may borrow it. Removing the line leaves the label on the --c-text-muted the
   * `.bs__lane` base already sets, so BOTH lane labels are now the same ink and
   * differ only in which end of the rail they sit at, which is the distinction
   * that is actually true.
   */
  .bs__lane--auth {
    margin-top: var(--stack-2);
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }

  /* `>>` and `<<`, never `→`. JetBrains Mono carries no arrow glyphs and the
     fallback renders as a stray `+`. */
  .bs__dir { letter-spacing: 0.06em; }

  /* ── The spine ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * IT STANDS ON A RAISED PANEL NOW. specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.4: seven
   * sections on one background is why the page has no depth to read, and the
   * answer measured against the closest dark comparator is a value ladder
   * rather than a lighter page. `sv-raised` is the third step, defined once in
   * styles/surfaces.css; nothing about it is restated here.
   *
   * THE RAIL IS WHY THE PANEL HAS BLOCK PADDING AND NO INLINE PADDING. Each
   * station draws its own third of the rail at 100% of its column, and the
   * outer two fade at the outer edge — that fade is what gives the rail its
   * raised look and it has to happen at the panel's own edge, not one gutter
   * inside it. Vertical padding is free; horizontal padding would move where
   * the rail ends and break the fade.
   */
  /* The raised panel. It holds the outer spacing and nothing else; every pixel
     of the rail's own geometry stays on the list below, so the split changes
     which element is which and changes no measurement. `figure.sv-raised`
     already has its user-agent margin zeroed in styles/surfaces.css. */
  .bs__panel { margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0; }

  .bs__spine {
    margin: 0;
    /* `padding-block` ALONE LEFT THE USER-AGENT'S 40px INLINE-START IN PLACE,
       and `list-style: none` removes the marker without removing the space it
       sat in. Measured at 1440 before this line: the three stations ran
       229 to 1251 inside a panel running 188 to 1252 — 40px of indent on the
       left, none on the right, so the drawing sat 20px off the centre of its own
       panel. Nobody chose that; it is the default showing through. The same leak
       was in `.spine` in MarketShape.astro and is fixed there too. */
    padding-block: var(--blk-pad);
    padding-inline: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /*
   * ROW ALIGNMENT WITHOUT SUBGRID. The four `auto` rows are fixed-height
   * decoration, identical in every station, and the stations stretch to a
   * common height as grid items. The two `1fr` rows therefore resolve to the
   * same height in all three columns, which puts the rail on one line however
   * the copy wraps.
   */
  .bs__station {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto auto auto auto 1fr;
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    /* Two stations' text must not touch at the column boundary. */
    padding-inline: var(--stack-1);
  }

  .bs__read {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 22ch;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.45;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .bs__read--sup {
    grid-row: 1;
    align-self: end;
    padding-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /*
   * ── THIS WAS TEAL AND THE ARGUMENT FOR IT DID NOT HOLD (A-104 item 7) ─────
   *
   * The line here read: "Teal is the verification colour and the authority is
   * the party verifying, so the lane colour is doing semantic work rather than
   * distinguishing two things by hue alone."
   *
   * Read what the lane actually says. "Publish the rubric", "Located word for
   * word", "Audit the commitment" — those are three things an authority DOES.
   * None of them is a figure the engine traced back to a recorded commitment,
   * which is the one thing --c-verified is allowed to mean. The comment was
   * reasoning from the party's job title to the token's meaning, and the token
   * is not named after a party. On the strict reading of one-semantic-per-hue
   * this was decoration in a mark's colour, spread over three of the six
   * readings in the section.
   *
   * --c-text-sub RATHER THAN --c-text, so the two lanes are still told apart
   * without a hue: the supplier reads at full ink from above, the authority at
   * secondary ink from below. That is a value step on the ladder the rest of
   * the page uses, and it survives greyscale exactly as the old teal did.
   *
   * THE ACCESSIBILITY ARGUMENT IS UNCHANGED AND WAS NEVER THE COLOUR'S. The
   * visually-hidden "Authority:" prefix carries the distinction with all colour
   * removed, WCAG 1.4.1, and it did that before this edit and does it after.
   *
   * IT IS ALSO WHAT LEAVES THE SECTION ONE FOCAL ACCENT. A-113: peak saturation
   * belongs to one point per section, and in this one that point is "or
   * dropped" in orchid — the only claim in the frame, and the only place the
   * page's colour vocabulary is spent here.
   */
  .bs__read--auth {
    grid-row: 6;
    align-self: start;
    padding-top: var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* Orchid = refused, sitewide, and it never appears decoratively. */
  .bs__refused { color: var(--c-refused); }

  /*
   * A HAIRLINE FROM EACH READING DOWN TO THE RAIL, FADING TOWARDS THE RAIL.
   *
   * TWO THINGS CHANGED HERE AND THEY ARE THE SAME CHANGE. The hue was
   * --grad-fade's default, --c-ambient-start, which is teal: a decorative
   * connector in the mark colour, which is the deployment A-113 objects to and
   * the rule this section is being brought back onto. It is --c-text at 30%
   * now, the same white the rail below it is made of, so the tick reads as part
   * of the drawn line rather than as a coloured pointer.
   *
   * AND THE ANGLE IS WRITTEN OUT, FOR THE REASON GIVEN AT THE RAIL ENDS ABOVE:
   * --grad-fade computes on :root with its fallbacks baked in, so
   * `--fade-angle: 180deg` and `--fade-angle: 0deg` were both inert and BOTH
   * ticks were rendering the same 90deg horizontal ramp across a 1px-wide
   * element. The up tick and the down tick have mirrored their whole lives in
   * the source and never once on the page. They mirror now.
   */
  .bs__tick {
    width: 1px;
    height: 26px;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent)
    );
  }

  .bs__tick--up { grid-row: 2; }

  .bs__tick--down {
    grid-row: 5;
    background: linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent)
    );
  }

  /*
   * THE RAIL IS DRAWN PER STATION AND MEETS ITSELF. Each cell's line is 100% of
   * its column, so three cells butt together into one continuous rail with no
   * absolute positioning and nothing to re-measure. The outer two fade at the
   * outer edge, which is what gives the rail its raised, lit look rather than
   * ending in two hard stops.
   */
  .bs__railcell {
    grid-row: 3;
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    /* Full column width, escaping the station's own inline padding. */
    width: calc(100% + 2 * var(--stack-1));
    height: 14px;
  }

  /*
   * THE RAIL IS NEUTRAL, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THIS RAIL AND THE
   * ONE TWO SECTIONS DOWN.
   *
   * It was `--c-verified` at 55%. ReasoningTrace's rail is teal too and KEEPS
   * its teal, and the two are not inconsistent: there, every dot on the rail is
   * a step the engine completed and the file says so in as many words, so teal
   * is asserting exactly what teal is for. Here the rail asserts SAMENESS — one
   * spine, two readers, opposite directions — and there is nothing on it that
   * has been verified. A hue that means one thing in one drawing and nothing in
   * the next is the hue losing its meaning, which is the whole of A-113.
   *
   * --c-text at 30% is not a new value. It is the neutral half of
   * ReasoningTrace's own rail ramp, reused rather than re-chosen, so the two
   * drawn rails on this page are made of the same white where they are made of
   * white at all.
   */
  .bs__railcell::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 50%;
    height: 1px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
  }

  /*
   * THE TWO OUTER ENDS FADE, AND THEY ARE WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN REACHED
   * THROUGH --grad-fade. THIS IS THE SAME TRAP `.bs__cap` BELOW ALREADY
   * RECORDS, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS EDIT COULD NOT BE A KNOB.
   *
   * A custom property's var()s are substituted where the property is DECLARED.
   * --grad-fade is declared on :root as
   *     linear-gradient(var(--fade-angle, 90deg), transparent,
   *       color-mix(in srgb, var(--fade-hue, var(--c-ambient-start))
   *                 var(--fade-strength, 55%), transparent))
   * so it computes ONCE, on :root, with all three fallbacks baked in, and
   * inherits finished. That is the measured behaviour tokens.css records for
   * --grad-ramp — "computes to linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)
   * on every element, whatever knobs it sets" — and there is no reason for
   * --grad-fade to behave differently.
   *
   * WHICH MEANS THESE TWO CELLS WERE TEAL AT 55% AND ONLY LOOKED CORRECT
   * BECAUSE THE MIDDLE CELL WAS TEAL AT 55% TOO. Neutralising the middle and
   * setting --fade-hue here would have left a white rail with a teal fade at
   * each end and nothing in the source to explain it.
   *
   * Every colour below still resolves from a token, which is what
   * check-design-system rule 7 asks of a gradient written at the point of use.
   * The 30% is the same 30% the middle cell takes, so the three cells are one
   * line made of one white.
   */
  .bs__station:first-child .bs__railcell::before {
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent)
    );
  }

  .bs__station:last-child .bs__railcell::before {
    background: linear-gradient(
      270deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent)
    );
  }

  /*
   * THE ISOMETRIC CAP. A flattened ellipse lit from above: a light-to-dark wash
   * rather than a flat fill, which is the single strongest tell separating a
   * drawn object from a PowerPoint shape. The halo puts it ON the rail rather
   * than in front of it.
   *
   * THE HALO WAS --c-verified-glow AND IS NOW WHITE. Same reasoning as the rail
   * it sits on: a station cap marks a moment in a contract, not a figure that
   * traced. The cap's own body is already made of --c-text at 62% falling to
   * 14%, so a white halo is the light the object was drawn in rather than a
   * second decision — and it is what leaves orchid on "or dropped" as the one
   * saturated thing in the frame.
   *
   * THE ALPHA IS NOT A GUESS AND IT IS NOT A MATCH EITHER, WHICH IS WORTH
   * SAYING. --c-verified-glow is teal at 0.45 and 22% white is not its
   * equivalent by any measurement I can make without a browser: white carries
   * more luminance per unit alpha than teal does on a near-black ground, so this
   * is chosen to sit BELOW the old value rather than beside it, on the same
   * restraint argument as everything else in this pass. If it reads too faint on
   * the built page, the knob is this number and the hue is settled.
   *
   * ── IT IS NOT A HAIRLINE, AND A GRADIENT AUDIT READ IT AS ONE ─────────────
   *
   * The 2026-08-05 gradient audit listed "62/14" here as the fourth of four
   * white-hairline recipes, alongside --grad-hairline's 30/5 and two 1px rules
   * on /crowmark, and recommended collapsing all four to the token. The other
   * three were collapsed on 2026-08-05. THIS ONE WAS NOT, and the reason is
   * visible in the ten lines above rather than arguable: a hairline is a 1px
   * rule whose gradient is an edge catching a light. This is a 22x10px ellipse
   * with a border-radius, a 1px ring and a 14px halo — a DRAWN OBJECT whose
   * body is a luminance ramp, which is the tell the note above says separates a
   * drawn object from a PowerPoint shape. Two white-to-white ramps are not the
   * same recipe because both ends are white; taking this to 30/5 would flatten
   * a cap on the homepage's rail to answer a finding about 1px rules on another
   * page. Recorded so the same collapse is not proposed a third time.
   */
  .bs__cap {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: 22px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    /* --grad-ramp: light-to-dark down the ellipse, which is the tell that
       separates a drawn object from a PowerPoint shape. */
    --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 62%, transparent);
    --ramp-to: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 14%, transparent);
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 62%, transparent),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 14%, transparent) 100%
    );
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 10%, transparent),
      0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 22%, transparent);
  }

  /* ══ M4 · TWO LIGHTS, ONE SPINE ═══════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `146:2` — "Two readers, opposite directions, meeting on the same
   * rail". TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.35.
   * CADENCE 3 beats, one per station, on the shared --m-beat.
   * PLAYS: ONCE, on reveal. It looped until 2026-08-05; see the header note.
   * REDUCED MOTION rail and caps at rest brightness, no travel.
   *
   * THE NUMBER DID NOT MOVE. This section already ran at 800ms per cell, which
   * is why --m-beat is 600ms and not 800: the grammar had to be a value the
   * whole page could live at rather than the largest one already in use, and at
   * 800 a five-step rail would take four seconds to cross. Three stations x
   * --m-beat is 1,800ms where it was 2,400ms, so the two lights are a third
   * quicker than they were and are now the same speed as every other light on
   * the page. See the motion grammar in styles/tokens.css.
   *
   * BOTH LIGHTS ARE WHITE, CORE AND BLOOM ALIKE, so neither can be read as a
   * marker at all. This line used to end "even though one is teal and the other
   * violet", which was true of the source and, it turns out, never true of the
   * page — see the note beside the beams themselves. `141:2` L04. The rail
   * underneath them is drawn at full strength from first paint and is never
   * touched.
   *
   * ONE DEVIATION FROM THE BOARD, AND IT IS THE HONEST ONE. `146:90` puts the
   * crossing at 1,100ms. Three equal cells cross at exactly one and a half
   * beats, and the board's own beats note says the cap bloom "fires on the
   * crossing, not on a timer". So the crossing wins and the bloom is at 900ms.
   */
  /*
   * THE TRACK EXISTS TO CLIP. Each beam is a full-width element translating
   * from -50% to +50%, so it is half a column outside its cell at each end of
   * the travel. Unclipped, that grows the page's scrollWidth for as long as the
   * light is moving — a horizontal-overflow failure that only exists mid-
   * animation and is invisible to any check that measures a settled page.
   *
   * The track is NOT the railcell itself. The railcell also holds .bs__cap,
   * whose 14px teal halo is drawn with a box-shadow, and clipping the railcell
   * would cut that halo off square.
   */
  .bs__track {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    /* Centred on the rail, with headroom either side for the beams' own glow.
       A 1px track would clip a 10px box-shadow to nothing. */
    top: 50%;
    height: 18px;
    margin-top: -9px;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .bs__beam {
    /* No animation: no pixels. The rest state of both lights is nothing. */
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .bs__beam {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      top: 9px;
      height: 1px;
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* The band is drawn in the middle of a full-width element and the element
       translates, so the travel is one compositor transform and the light
       scales with the column rather than being a fixed px width that reads
       wide on a phone. */
    /*
     * ── THE TWO LIGHTS ARE NOW THE SAME LIGHT, AND THAT IS THE FIX ──────────
     *
     * This comment used to read: "The two lights differ in exactly one thing,
     * their hue, which is the section's whole argument: two readers travelling
     * in opposite directions on the same rail."
     *
     * Read it again. The argument named in that sentence is DIRECTION, and the
     * sentence then says the difference is HUE. Light A was teal and light B
     * was violet, so the section spent both of the page's semantic colours —
     * verified and refused — on two objects that assert neither, in the one
     * section that also holds the page's single legitimate orchid, "or
     * dropped". A reader who has learned that orchid means refused meets a
     * violet light sweeping the rail eleven words above it.
     *
     * Both are white lamps now: the core and the bloom are the same --c-text,
     * which is what styles/tokens.css calls a LAMP as opposed to a marker. A
     * marker is a flat coloured stroke; a lamp is a hot white core with the hue
     * in its bloom, and a lamp with NO hue at all cannot be misread as
     * labelling what it passes over. What separates A from B is the only thing
     * that ever carried the argument, which is which way each one is going.
     *
     * ── AND IT IS WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN SET THROUGH --beam-hue ────────────
     *
     * The obvious edit is `--beam-hue: var(--c-text); background:
     * var(--grad-beam)`. It would have changed nothing, for the reason recorded
     * at the rail ends above and at `.bs__cap` below: --grad-beam is declared on
     * :root and a custom property's var()s are substituted where the property is
     * DECLARED, so it computes once with --beam-hue's fallback baked in and
     * inherits finished.
     *
     * WHICH MEANS LIGHT B WAS NEVER VIOLET. `--beam-hue: var(--c-ambient-end)`
     * on `.bs__beam--b` has been inert for as long as it has existed, so the two
     * lights have both been rendering the token's default teal, and the comment
     * this block replaces was describing a difference that was in the source and
     * not on the page. That does not change the decision — a teal lamp asserts
     * verification just as wrongly as a violet one asserts refusal — but it does
     * change what "before" looked like, and it is the reason this is written as
     * a literal gradient: a knob nobody can see working is not a fix.
     *
     * THE GEOMETRY IS THE TOKEN'S, UNCHANGED: the same 16% default half-width,
     * the same 0.375 bloom stop, the same 70% bloom alpha, the same 90deg axis.
     * Every colour resolves from a token, which is what check-design-system
     * rule 7 asks of a gradient written at the point of use.
     */
    .bs__beam--a,
    .bs__beam--b {
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 34%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 70%, transparent) 44%,
        var(--c-text) 50%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 70%, transparent) 56%,
        transparent 66%
      );
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
    }

    /* ONE PASS EACH, ON REVEAL. These were `var(--m-cycle) ... infinite`: three
       beats of travel followed by seventeen beats of nothing, then the whole
       thing again, for as long as the page was open. A-104 and A-111 between
       them end that; see the note above the keyframes for the arithmetic, which
       is unchanged to the millisecond. The duration is now one beat because one
       beat is what a beam is actually visible for, and an animation that spends
       seventeen twentieths of its life holding a rest state is one
       document.getAnimations() goes on counting. */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__beam--a {
      animation: bs-beam-a var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .bs__beam--b {
      animation: bs-beam-b var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    /* A takes the stations left to right; B takes them right to left. One
       clock, mirrored, so the two are always the same distance from centre and
       meet dead centre at one and a half beats, which is where the cap bloom
       below is delayed to.

       THIS NOTE USED TO ARGUE THE MIRROR FROM THE REPEAT — "a delay on an
       infinite animation is a permanent phase offset, so the mirror holds on
       every repeat rather than only on the first". There is no repeat now, so
       what the delays buy is the one thing they always bought: the stagger
       itself. The four values are untouched. */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(2) .bs__beam--a { animation-delay: var(--m-beat); }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(3) .bs__beam--a { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2); }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(2) .bs__beam--b { animation-delay: var(--m-beat); }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(1) .bs__beam--b { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2); }

    /* Evaluation only, on the crossing at one and a half beats. The beat the
       standfirst used to have to describe in words: the same measures,
       whichever chair you read them from.

       ONCE, LIKE THE TWO LIGHTS IT ANSWERS. It looped on the same twenty-beat
       cycle, so the middle cap re-bloomed every twelve seconds whether or not
       anything was crossing it — the crossing is the event, and an event that
       re-stages itself on a timer is a performance. The gesture is 300ms end to
       end, which is the 7.5% -> 10% window it always occupied, and it starts at
       exactly the beat the two beams meet. */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station[data-mid] .bs__cap {
      animation: bs-cap 300ms var(--m-ease-land) calc(var(--m-beat) * 1.5) 1;
    }

    /*
     * THE REFUSAL, AND IT STAYS A MARKER — AND IT IS THE ONE THING IN THIS
     * SECTION THAT STILL PLAYS ONCE. "or dropped" takes a 400ms orchid glow as
     * the two lights cross, which is the only orchid in the frame. It is a flat
     * orchid word with a glow BEHIND it and never a white-cored light: that is
     * the whole distinction the page teaches, and this section is where it is
     * spent.
     *
     * IT NEVER LOOPED, AND AS OF 2026-08-05 NOTHING AROUND IT DOES EITHER.
     * This paragraph used to open "WHY IT DOES NOT LOOP WHEN EVERYTHING AROUND
     * IT NOW DOES", and the argument it made was that a refusal re-announcing
     * itself every twelve seconds is a performance, where light that repeats is
     * only light. That argument was right and it has simply been extended: the
     * owner's instruction of 2026-08-05 stops the light repeating too, so the
     * split this rule used to be the exception to is gone and everything in the
     * section fires once. The word, its colour and its contrast are of course
     * present from first paint either way — this only ever decided whether a
     * glow behind it repeats.
     *
     * THE TEXT'S OWN OPACITY AND COLOUR ARE UNTOUCHED. The board specifies
     * 0.5 -> 0.95 -> 0.6, which would leave the word dimmer at the end than the
     * rest state it started from and breaks binding rule 02 on its own board.
     * A text-shadow rises from nothing and returns to nothing instead, so the
     * word measures identically against the background at every frame.
     */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__refused {
      animation: bs-refused 400ms var(--m-ease-land) calc(var(--m-beat) * 2.5) 1;
    }
  }

  /* ONE BEAT OF TRAVEL PER CELL, AND THE BLOCK IS NOW THAT BEAT AND NOTHING
     ELSE. A beam's window was 0% to 5% of a twenty-beat cycle with the last
     fifteen sixteenths of the block holding it parked off the far edge; the
     duration is one beat now, so 0.6% of the cycle is 12% of the pass and 4.4%
     is 88%. Every fade lands at the same instant it did. The stagger is
     untouched and is still what puts the three cells in order.

     THE LAST OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE RESTING STATE. Each beam starts
     translated off one edge at opacity 0 and ends translated off the OTHER edge
     at opacity 0: it crosses and leaves, and never resolves to where the
     element rests. That is what keeps scripts/check-motion.js rule 2 from
     reading a travelling light as an entrance that belongs in motion.css.
     Anything that "tidies" a final offset to translateX(0) fails that gate. */
  @keyframes bs-beam-a {
    0%    { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
    12%   { opacity: 1; }
    88%   { opacity: 1; }
    100%  { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  @keyframes bs-beam-b {
    0%    { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
    12%   { opacity: 1; }
    88%   { opacity: 1; }
    100%  { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* Scale about the cap's own centre. It is already translate(-50%,-50%) for
     positioning, so the scale has to be composed with that translate or the
     cap jumps a half-width sideways the moment the animation starts.

     THE BLOOM IS THE WHOLE BLOCK NOW. It used to sit inside a twenty-beat cycle
     as 7.5% -> 8.75% -> 10%, which is a 300ms gesture beginning at one and a
     half beats. The delay carries the "beginning at one and a half beats" and
     the duration carries the 300ms, so the peak is at the midpoint here. Same
     gesture, same length, same moment.

     THE HALO IS WHITE, LIKE THE CAP'S REST STATE. It was --c-verified-glow, and
     a cap is a moment on a rail rather than a figure that traced. */
  @keyframes bs-cap {
    0%   { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
    50%  {
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.08);
      box-shadow:
        0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 10%, transparent),
        0 0 22px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
    }
    100% { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
  }

  @keyframes bs-refused {
    0%   { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 0%, transparent); }
    45%  { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 62%, transparent); }
    100% { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 0%, transparent); }
  }

  /* ══ THE STATION ANSWERS A CURSOR ═════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * One :hover rule in this section before today, and it was on a link.
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6 is the reason this block exists.
   *
   * WHAT A STATION DOES UNDER THE POINTER: the cap blooms and its halo opens,
   * both ticks brighten, the rail segment brightens, and the name chip lifts
   * onto the fourth surface step with its border going to the glow colour.
   * The two READINGS are untouched, because they are the section's content and
   * a reader must never be given a reason to think a hover changed what a
   * commitment says.
   *
   * THE CAP SCALES, WHICH IS THE ONE TRANSFORM IN THIS BLOCK, AND IT IS ON
   * DECORATION. It is composed with the translate that positions it, exactly as
   * `bs-cap` already does, because a bare scale would jump it half a width
   * sideways. styles/surfaces.css strips transforms under reduced motion.
   *
   * THE CAP BLOOM NO LONGER OWNS `transform` FOR THE REST OF THE PAGE'S LIFE.
   * This used to read: "`bs-cap` runs infinitely on [data-mid], so the hover
   * scale there is overridden while the animation runs". That was a real and
   * permanent conflict — a running animation owns a property outright, so the
   * middle station's hover scale never once took effect. `bs-cap` is a 300ms
   * one-shot as of 2026-08-05, so the conflict lasts 300ms after reveal and
   * then the middle station hovers exactly like the other two. The halo opened
   * either way; what is recovered is the scale.
   *
   * NO COLOUR ON THIS PAGE CHANGES MEANING HERE. Orchid stays on "or dropped"
   * and nothing below touches it. The cap, the ticks and the rail all answer in
   * white now, which is the same white they are drawn in, so a hover brightens
   * the drawing rather than colouring it.
   */
  .bs__cap,
  .bs__tick,
  .bs__railcell::before,
  .bs__chip {
    transition:
      background-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur) var(--ease),
      transform var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    /* The halo opens white, which is the colour it now has at rest. It was
       --c-verified-glow here as well as at rest, so the pair were consistent
       and consistently wrong; check-palette-roles rule 2 would have fired the
       moment only one of them moved, because an interactive state may restate a
       mark the subject already has and may never introduce one. Both moved. */
    .bs__station:hover .bs__cap {
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.14);
      box-shadow:
        0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 18%, transparent),
        0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 34%, transparent);
    }

    .bs__station:hover .bs__tick { filter: brightness(1.8); }

    .bs__station:hover .bs__railcell::before { filter: brightness(1.8); }

    .bs__station:hover .bs__chip {
      background-color: var(--c-lit);
      border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
      box-shadow: var(--gloss-inset), var(--shadow-ladder);
    }

    .bs__more a:hover { color: var(--c-text); }
  }

  .bs__chip {
    grid-row: 4;
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    background: var(--c-card);
  }

  /* ── Close ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The closing line and the mono proof row are gone, and their rules with
     them: see the note above STATIONS for why each went and where its content
     now lives. The links close the section directly, so they take the section
     gap the foot used to. */
  .bs__more {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0 var(--stack-2);
  }

  .bs__more a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  /* THICKNESS, NOT COLOUR. The rest state is --c-interactive, which is #FFFFFF
     under V6, so there is no brighter value for a hover to reach; changing hue
     instead would move the link onto a marker and say it had been verified.
     styles/prose.css:128 reached the same answer for the site's highest-volume
     link, and this is that answer rather than a second one. */
  .bs__more a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }

  .bs__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /*
   * THE LINEAR FALLBACK, AND IT IS THE SHIPPED LAYOUT AT 390px. Three columns
   * of two-line labels cannot hold a legible rail at that width: each column
   * gets ~110px and the station names alone wrap to three lines. Below 720px
   * the spine turns 90 degrees and becomes a vertical one — the rail runs down
   * the left edge as a border, each station keeps its cap, and supplier and
   * authority readings stack under their own station rather than being split
   * across the page. The geometry changes; not one word does.
   */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .bs__panel { margin-top: var(--stack-2); }

    .bs__spine {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--stack-2);
      /* The vertical rail. One line down the whole list, drawn once, and it is
         the same rail as the horizontal one above the breakpoint — so it takes
         the same white at the same 30% rather than the teal it used to, which
         would otherwise have made the phone layout the last teal rail on the
         page. */
      border-left: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
      padding-left: var(--stack-2);
    }

    .bs__station {
      grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
      justify-items: start;
      text-align: left;
      padding-inline: 0;
      position: relative;
    }

    /* The ticks and the horizontal rail cell have nothing to connect once the
       spine is vertical; the border on the list is the rail now. */
    .bs__tick { display: none; }

    .bs__railcell {
      grid-row: auto;
      /* Sits on the vertical rail, level with its own station name. */
      position: absolute;
      left: calc(-1 * var(--stack-2));
      top: 0.7em;
      width: auto;
      height: auto;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }

    /* The horizontal segment has no neighbours to meet once the list is
       vertical, and left in place it draws a 10px stub through the cap. */
    .bs__railcell::before { display: none; }

    /* Neither light has a rail to travel once the spine is vertical: the rail
       is the list's left border now, and a horizontal beam inside a cap-sized
       box would be a 10px flicker beside each station. The cap bloom and the
       refusal glow both stay; neither needs the geometry. */
    .bs__track { display: none; }

    .bs__cap {
      position: static;
      transform: none;
      display: block;
      /* Upright rather than flattened: a horizontal ellipse on a vertical rail
         reads as a break in the line instead of a station on it. */
      width: 10px;
      height: 10px;
    }

    .bs__chip { grid-row: 1; margin-top: 0; }

    .bs__read { grid-row: auto; max-width: 40ch; }

    .bs__read--sup {
      grid-row: 2;
      align-self: auto;
      padding: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    }

    .bs__read--auth {
      grid-row: 3;
      align-self: auto;
      padding: 4px 0 0;
    }

    .bs__lane--auth { justify-content: flex-start; }

    .bs__more { justify-content: flex-start; }
